//! lotus/stereo_rectify.rye — ALES138: the full-wave rectifier carried into stereo, ONE SHARED FOLD AT ZERO, the EVEN corner of the stereo NONLINEAR class. ALES84 named `rectify` — y = |x|, the plainest even
//! generator: fold the whole negative half of the wave up onto the positive side, turning a symmetric wave into a one-sided, DC-heavy signal at DOUBLE the frequency (the diode bridge, the octave-up fuzz). Where
//! ALES132–ALES137 shape or hold a wave through ODD and NOT-ODD maps — the hard clip, soft clip, wavefold, tube, bit-crush, decimator — the rectifier is the complement: it takes the ABSOLUTE VALUE, the purest
//! EVEN map, |−x| = |x|. Yet a Lotus master is a StereoClip (ALES10), two Clips heard together whose defining invariant is that LEFT and RIGHT hold the SAME length. This rung carries the rectifier into stereo
//! the plain way: run ALES84's proven mono `rectify` on each channel over the SAME span, so one fold at zero lands on BOTH speakers together.
//!
//! The rectifier is the STRONGEST image-break in the class — the honest opposite of the decimator (ALES137). The decimator copies an anchor and so PRESERVED the stereo image; crush and tube (ALES135/136)
//! computed new values and BROKE the antisymmetry; the rectifier, an EVEN map, COLLAPSES it. An out-of-phase master (right the exact negation of left) rectified through the same fold comes back with
//! right = |−left| = |left| = left: the two channels become IDENTICAL, the stereo image folded down to mono on the magnitude. That is not a bug to design around; it is the full-wave rectifier's whole point read
//! in the stereo field — the even map lands +a and −a on the same a, so an antisymmetric stereo pair lands on one signal. Yet the RATIO holds: |2x| = 2|x|, so a 1:2 master keeps its 1:2 ratio (both sides
//! non-negative after), and an identical-channel master stays identical.
//!
//! The rule, stated once: the mono rectifier carries NO gain, NO ceiling, and NO resolution — an absolute value has no parameter that could be illegal — so this is the PLAINEST lift in the whole class, naming
//! exactly one fault. Refuse BadRange (a span outside the samples) BEFORE either channel is mutated — the check depends only on the shared length, so it is made ONCE up front, and a refusal never rectifies one
//! channel and leaves the other signed; otherwise rectify both channels over [start, start+count) via ALES84's `rectify`. RectifyError reused whole — the stereo lift adds no fault.
//!
//! The crux — four laws. THE STEREO RECTIFY LAW: each channel equals ALES84's mono `rectify` over the same span byte for byte — every output is |x| pinned to the rail (sample_min's magnitude 32768 saturating to
//! sample_max, the one place |x| leaves the i16 rail). THE BALANCE / LENGTH LAW: left.len == right.len after the edit and both hold their starting length (a rectify writes values only). THE IMAGE-COLLAPSE LAW:
//! because |x| is an EVEN map, an out-of-phase master (right = −left) comes back with IDENTICAL channels (right = |−left| = |left| = left), the antisymmetry collapsed to mono rather than merely broken; yet the
//! ratio holds (|2x| = 2|x|, a 1:2 master keeping its ratio), an identical-channel master stays identical, every output on both channels is NON-NEGATIVE, and the map is idempotent (twice equals once). THE
//! ATOMICITY / DEGENERATE LAW: a refusal (BadRange) leaves BOTH channels byte for byte untouched and still balanced; count = 0 the identity on both.
//!
//! Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season C thread (Lotus · the creative suite) · waymark ALES · rung ALES138.
//! Design read: active-designing/date/20260815/20260815-040027_fill-ales138-lotus-stereo-rectify.md
//! Stands on: lotus/rectify.rye (ALES84) — the mono full-wave rectifier run once per channel, |x| pinned to the rail; lotus/stereo_decimate.rye (ALES137) — the image-PRESERVING member whose opposite pole this
//! rung is; lotus/pan.rye (ALES10) — the StereoClip (two mono Clips, left/right) and its balance invariant; lotus/timeline.rye (ALES2) — the Clip, its buf/len/samples, sample_min/max.
//!
//! HONEST SCOPE — software only, purely LOCAL. Two bounded in-process i16 Clips (left, right) on one bench, siloed to lotus/. It changes sample VALUES only through ALES84's own rectify, each output at most its
//! input's magnitude, never a length; the shape is a full-wave absolute value, instantaneous (no attack/release, no anti-aliasing). No real sample rate, no network, no keys, no funds, no real device, no real
//! speaker.
//!
//!   env RYE_ZIG=vendor/zig-toolchain/zig rye/bin/rye run lotus/stereo_rectify.rye selftest

const std = @import("std");
const assert = std.debug.assert;
const print = std.debug.print;

const timeline = @import("timeline.rye");
const pan = @import("pan.rye");
const rectify = @import("rectify.rye");

pub const stereo_rectify_version = "20260815.040027";

/// Stereo rectify's faults are exactly ALES84's own `RectifyError` — `BadRange` (a span outside the samples) — reused by name, since the stereo lift adds no new fault: it only runs the same rectify on each
/// channel after one shared validation. The rectifier names no other fault (an absolute value has no illegal parameter).
pub const StereoRectifyError = rectify.RectifyError;

/// Rectify `[start, start+count)` in BOTH channels of `sc`, the SAME fold at zero on each, running ALES84's proven mono `rectify`. Each sample becomes its absolute value `|x|` — the negative half of the wave
/// folded up onto the positive side, exact for every i16 except sample_min, whose magnitude 32768 overflows the positive rail and saturates to sample_max. Validates the span once against the shared length before
/// either channel is mutated: refuses `BadRange`, both channels untouched. Values only — never the length; the two channels leave balanced and both non-negative over the span.
pub fn stereo_rectify(sc: *pan.StereoClip, start: u32, count: u32) StereoRectifyError!void {
    // invariant: the two channels enter balanced — the defining StereoClip invariant, the ground the edit preserves.
    assert(sc.left.len == sc.right.len);

    // invariant: the span is legal — refused by name before any write, so a refusal never rectifies one channel and leaves the other signed. The span check depends only on the shared length, made once up front;
    // it mirrors ALES84's own guard exactly, so each mono rectify below is pre-validated to succeed. The rectifier carries no other parameter, so this lone check is the whole gate.
    const len: u32 = sc.left.len;
    if (start > len) return error.BadRange;
    if (count > len - start) return error.BadRange;

    // Every check passed and holds identically for both channels, so each mono rectify is pre-validated to succeed — no refusal can land between the two channel edits. The SAME fold at zero on each.
    try rectify.rectify(&sc.left, start, count);
    try rectify.rectify(&sc.right, start, count);

    // invariant: the channels leave balanced — a rectify writes values only, so each channel holds its starting length and the stereo image stays aligned in time.
    assert(sc.left.len == sc.right.len);
    assert(sc.left.len <= sc.left.buf.len and sc.right.len <= sc.right.buf.len);
}

// --- selftest: THE STEREO RECTIFY LAW — each channel equals ALES84's mono rectify over the same span byte for byte; THE BALANCE / LENGTH LAW — left.len == right.len after, both holding their starting length;
//     THE IMAGE-COLLAPSE LAW — an OUT-OF-PHASE master (right = −left) comes back with IDENTICAL channels (the even map collapses antisymmetry to mono), yet a 1:2 master KEEPS its ratio, an identical-channel
//     master stays identical, every output NON-NEGATIVE on both, the map idempotent; THE ATOMICITY / DEGENERATE LAW — a refusal leaves both channels untouched and balanced, count = 0 the identity ----

fn eq(got: []const i16, want: []const i16) bool {
    if (got.len != want.len) return false;
    var i: u32 = 0;
    while (i < want.len) : (i += 1) if (got[i] != want[i]) return false;
    return true;
}

fn load_stereo(sc: *pan.StereoClip, l: []const i16, r: []const i16) !void {
    try timeline.splice(&sc.left, 0, l);
    try timeline.splice(&sc.right, 0, r);
}

fn run_selftest() !u8 {
    // 1. THE STEREO RECTIFY LAW — each channel equals ALES84's mono rectify over the same span, proven side by side with different per-channel samples; negatives fold to their magnitude, the rail edge saturates
    //    sample_min to sample_max, non-negatives pass unchanged.
    {
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &[_]i16{ -300, 300, timeline.sample_min, -2000 }, &[_]i16{ 100, -50, -32767, 2500 });
        try stereo_rectify(&sc, 0, 4);

        var ml = timeline.Clip{};
        try timeline.splice(&ml, 0, &[_]i16{ -300, 300, timeline.sample_min, -2000 });
        try rectify.rectify(&ml, 0, 4);
        var mr = timeline.Clip{};
        try timeline.splice(&mr, 0, &[_]i16{ 100, -50, -32767, 2500 });
        try rectify.rectify(&mr, 0, 4);

        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), ml.samples())) return error.LeftChannelWrong;
        if (!eq(sc.right.samples(), mr.samples())) return error.RightChannelWrong;
        // by hand: |−300|=300, |300|=300, |−32768|=32767 (saturated), |−2000|=2000 / |100|=100, |−50|=50, |−32767|=32767, |2500|=2500.
        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), &[_]i16{ 300, 300, 32767, 2000 })) return error.LeftHandWrong;
        if (!eq(sc.right.samples(), &[_]i16{ 100, 50, 32767, 2500 })) return error.RightHandWrong;
    }

    // 2. THE BALANCE / LENGTH LAW — the channels stay equal length and each holds its starting length; a rectify writes values only.
    {
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &[_]i16{ 100, -200, 300, -400, 500 }, &[_]i16{ -500, 400, -300, 200, -100 });
        try stereo_rectify(&sc, 0, 5);
        if (sc.left.len != sc.right.len) return error.Unbalanced;
        if (sc.left.len != 5) return error.LengthChanged;
    }

    // 3a. THE IMAGE-COLLAPSE LAW — an OUT-OF-PHASE master (right the exact negation of left) rectified through the same fold comes back with IDENTICAL channels, because the even map lands +a and −a on the same a:
    //     right[i] = |−left[i]| = |left[i]| = left[i] (after both are non-negative). The antisymmetry is not merely broken (crush/tube) but COLLAPSED to mono — the honest opposite of the decimator, which preserved
    //     the image.
    {
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &[_]i16{ 100, -300, 500, -700 }, &[_]i16{ -100, 300, -500, 700 }); // right = −left, out of phase
        try stereo_rectify(&sc, 0, 4);
        const l = sc.left.samples();
        const r = sc.right.samples();
        var i: u32 = 0;
        while (i < 4) : (i += 1) {
            if (r[i] != l[i]) return error.AntisymmetryNotCollapsed; // the channels are now IDENTICAL — the image folded to mono
        }
        if (!eq(l, &[_]i16{ 100, 300, 500, 700 })) return error.CollapseMagnitudeWrong;
    }

    // 3b. THE IMAGE-COLLAPSE LAW, the RATIO HOLDS — a 1:2 master (right twice left) keeps its 1:2 ratio at each index, because |2x| = 2|x|; where the antisymmetry collapses, the magnitude ratio survives.
    {
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &[_]i16{ -1000, 400, -700, 200 }, &[_]i16{ -2000, 800, -1400, 400 }); // right = 2·left
        try stereo_rectify(&sc, 0, 4);
        const l = sc.left.samples();
        const r = sc.right.samples();
        var i: u32 = 0;
        while (i < 4) : (i += 1) {
            if (@as(i32, r[i]) != 2 * @as(i32, l[i])) return error.RatioBroke; // 1:2 held at every index, both non-negative
        }
    }

    // 3c. EVERY OUTPUT NON-NEGATIVE ON BOTH, IDEMPOTENT, AND AN IDENTICAL-CHANNEL MASTER STAYS IDENTICAL — a rectifier makes a one-sided signal, so no output on either channel is below zero; rectifying twice
    //     equals once (a non-negative sample rectifies to itself); and a shared fold on equal channels gives equal channels.
    {
        var once = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&once, &[_]i16{ 700, -1500, 4000, -90, 12 }, &[_]i16{ -4000, 900, -700, 33, -8 });
        try stereo_rectify(&once, 0, 5);
        const l = once.left.samples();
        const r = once.right.samples();
        var i: u32 = 0;
        while (i < 5) : (i += 1) {
            if (l[i] < 0 or r[i] < 0) return error.NegativeOutput;
        }
        var twice = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&twice, &[_]i16{ 700, -1500, 4000, -90, 12 }, &[_]i16{ -4000, 900, -700, 33, -8 });
        try stereo_rectify(&twice, 0, 5);
        try stereo_rectify(&twice, 0, 5);
        if (!eq(once.left.samples(), twice.left.samples())) return error.LeftNotIdempotent;
        if (!eq(once.right.samples(), twice.right.samples())) return error.RightNotIdempotent;

        var same = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&same, &[_]i16{ -500, 3000, -500, 3000 }, &[_]i16{ -500, 3000, -500, 3000 });
        try stereo_rectify(&same, 0, 4);
        if (!eq(same.left.samples(), same.right.samples())) return error.IdentityChannelsSplit;
    }

    // 3d. THE SPAN DISCIPLINE — rectifying a sub-span leaves samples outside [start, count) exactly as they were on both channels, negatives among them preserved (the in-place discipline the whole suite keeps).
    {
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &[_]i16{ -100, -200, -500, 500, -300, -400 }, &[_]i16{ 400, 300, -500, 500, -300, -400 });
        try stereo_rectify(&sc, 2, 2); // indices 2,3 only, both channels
        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), &[_]i16{ -100, -200, 500, 500, -300, -400 })) return error.LeftSpanLeaked;
        if (!eq(sc.right.samples(), &[_]i16{ 400, 300, 500, 500, -300, -400 })) return error.RightSpanLeaked;
    }

    // 4a. THE ATOMICITY LAW, bad span — a span past the samples refuses BadRange, both channels byte for byte untouched and still balanced. The rectifier names no other fault (an absolute value has no illegal
    //     parameter), so this lone refusal is the whole atomicity story.
    {
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &[_]i16{ 5, -6, 7 }, &[_]i16{ -7, 6, -5 });
        if (stereo_rectify(&sc, 1, 3)) |_| return error.BadRangeSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadRange) return error.WrongBadRange;
        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), &[_]i16{ 5, -6, 7 })) return error.BadRangeLeftMutated;
        if (!eq(sc.right.samples(), &[_]i16{ -7, 6, -5 })) return error.BadRangeRightMutated;
        if (sc.left.len != sc.right.len) return error.BadRangeUnbalanced;
    }

    // 4b. THE DEGENERATE LAW — count = 0 is the identity on both channels (an empty span folds nothing).
    {
        var noop = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&noop, &[_]i16{ 7, -8, 9 }, &[_]i16{ -7, 8, -9 });
        try stereo_rectify(&noop, 1, 0); // empty span — identity
        if (!eq(noop.left.samples(), &[_]i16{ 7, -8, 9 })) return error.ZeroCountLeftChanged;
        if (!eq(noop.right.samples(), &[_]i16{ -7, 8, -9 })) return error.ZeroCountRightChanged;
    }

    print("GREEN ales-stereo-rectify: the FULL-WAVE RECTIFIER CARRIED INTO STEREO, ONE SHARED FOLD AT ZERO, the EVEN corner of the stereo NONLINEAR class — rectify a span in both channels of a master, running " ++
        "ALES84's proven mono rectify on each (y = |x|, the negative half folded up onto the positive side, sample_min saturating to the rail), so one fold at zero lands on both speakers together. A Lotus " ++
        "master is a StereoClip (ALES10) whose defining invariant is that left and right hold the same length. Where ALES132–ALES137 shape or hold a wave through odd and not-odd maps, the rectifier is the " ++
        "complement — the purest EVEN map, |−x| = |x| — and the STRONGEST image-break in the class: the honest opposite of the decimator, which copied an anchor and preserved the image. Because the map is even, " ++
        "an out-of-phase master (right = −left) comes back with IDENTICAL channels (right = |−left| = |left| = left), the antisymmetry not merely broken but COLLAPSED to mono; yet the ratio holds (|2x| = 2|x|, " ++
        "a 1:2 master keeping its ratio), an identical-channel master stays identical, every output on both channels is non-negative, and the map is idempotent (twice equals once). The mono rectifier carries no " ++
        "gain, no ceiling, no resolution, so this is the plainest lift in the class, naming exactly one fault: it validates the span once against the shared length before either channel is mutated (BadRange on a " ++
        "span past the samples), so a refusal never rectifies one channel and leaves the other signed; RectifyError reused whole. The crux is four laws. THE STEREO RECTIFY LAW: each channel equals ALES84's mono " ++
        "rectify over the same span byte for byte. THE BALANCE / LENGTH LAW: left.len == right.len after and both hold their starting length. THE IMAGE-COLLAPSE LAW: an out-of-phase master returns identical " ++
        "(the even map collapses antisymmetry to mono), the ratio held, an identical master staying identical, every output non-negative, the map idempotent. THE ATOMICITY / DEGENERATE LAW: a refusal leaves " ++
        "both channels untouched and balanced, count = 0 the identity. Purely local — two bounded i16 Clips rectified through the proven mono rectify; no real sample rate, no network, no keys, no funds, no real " ++
        "speaker\n", .{});
    return 0;
}

pub fn main(init: std.process.Init) !u8 {
    _ = init;
    return run_selftest();
}
